1
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:12,780
And Hermes of Cyllene was calling out the souls
of the suitor-men. He held in his hands the rod,

2
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,420
beautiful, golden, with which he charms men's eyes

3
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:20,620
to sleep, whose eyes he wills,
and wakes others from their slumber;

4
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:25,260
with this he set them moving and led them,
and they followed, squeaking.

5
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:30,000
As when bats in the corner of a wondrous
cave flit squeaking, when one drops off

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,020
the chain of them clinging to the rock and to each other:

7
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:39,000
so these went with him, squeaking; and Hermes
the kindly led them down along the dank ways.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,180
Past the streams of Ocean they went, past the White Rock,

9
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:47,540
past the gates of the Sun and the country of dreams,
and soon they came to the meadow of asphodel,

10
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:51,680
where the souls have their dwelling —
the phantoms of the worn-out dead.

11
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:57,600
There they found the soul of Achilles, Peleus' son,
and of Patroclus, and of blameless Antilochus,

12
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:04,280
and of Ajax, who was first in beauty and in build of
all the Danaans after the blameless son of Peleus.

13
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:10,360
These were gathered around Achilles; and close
to them came the soul of Agamemnon, Atreus' son,

14
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,560
grieving; and around him others
were gathered — all who died

15
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,140
beside him in Aegisthus' house, and met their fate.

16
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:19,880
And the soul of Peleus' son spoke to him first:

17
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:25,700
"Son of Atreus, we used to say that you, of all the
hero-men, were dear to Zeus who delights in thunder

18
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:29,120
all your days — because you were lord over many strong men

19
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:32,720
in the land of the Trojans, where we Achaeans suffered.

20
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:38,400
And yet it was to you that ruinous doom would
come so early — the doom no man born escapes.

21
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,280
If only, in the fullness of the honor you were lord of,

22
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:44,400
you had met your death and fate
in the land of the Trojans:

23
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,980
then all the Achaeans together would have made your tomb,

24
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,500
and you would have won great fame for your son hereafter.

25
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,100
As it was, your portion was to be
caught by the most pitiful of deaths."

26
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,820
Then the soul of Atreus' son answered him:

27
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:04,380
"Blessed son of Peleus, Achilles like the gods,
who died at Troy, far from Argos — and around you the others

28
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:07,700
fell, the best of the sons of Trojans and Achaeans,

29
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:10,580
fighting over your body; and you in the swirl of the dust

30
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:13,960
lay great in your greatness, your horsemanship forgotten.

31
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:16,360
And we fought the whole day through, and would never

32
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:20,420
have ceased from the fighting,
had Zeus not stopped us with a storm.

33
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:23,240
And when we had carried you
out of the battle to the ships,

34
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:25,920
we laid you on a bier, and cleansed your beautiful body

35
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:31,600
with warm water and with oil; and over you the
Danaans shed many hot tears, and cut their hair.

36
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:34,600
And out of the sea your mother came,
with the deathless sea-women,

37
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:38,000
when she heard the news; and a cry went up over the deep,

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,780
unearthly — and trembling took hold of all the Achaeans.

39
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:43,760
They would have sprung up then
and fled to the hollow ships,

40
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:47,580
had not a man held them back, one deep in old wisdom:

41
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,640
Nestor, whose counsel had shown truest long before.

42
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:53,180
With good will toward them he spoke out and said:

43
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:58,400
'Hold, Argives! Do not run, young men of the Achaeans!

44
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:04,380
It is his mother coming out of the sea, with the
deathless sea-women, to look on the face of her dead son.'

45
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:07,860
So he spoke, and the great-hearted
Achaeans checked their flight.

46
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,600
And around you stood the daughters
of the old man of the sea,

47
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,580
mourning piteously; and they wrapped
you in imperishable clothing.

48
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:22,420
And the Muses, all nine, answering voice to lovely voice,
sang the dirge. Not one Argive could you have seen

49
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:26,360
unweeping then, so keen was the clear Muse's song.

50
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:32,020
Seventeen days together, nights and days alike,
we mourned you — deathless gods and mortal men;

51
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:34,920
on the eighteenth we gave you to the fire, and around you

52
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:38,280
slaughtered fat sheep in plenty and spiral-horned cattle.

53
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:43,960
You burned in the clothing of the gods, in oil in
plenty and in sweet honey; and many Achaean heroes

54
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:49,980
moved in armor around your burning pyre,
men on foot and horsemen, and a great roar went up.

55
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:55,620
And when the flame of Hephaestus had made an end of you,
at dawn we gathered your white bones, Achilles,

56
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:01,900
into unmixed wine and oil. Your mother gave
a golden two-eared jar — a gift of Dionysus,

57
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,680
she said, and the work of famous Hephaestus.

58
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:11,320
In that lie your white bones, shining Achilles,
mingled with the bones of dead Patroclus, Menoetius' son,

59
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:17,420
and apart lie Antilochus', whom you honored beyond
all your other companions after Patroclus died.

60
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:22,580
And over them all we heaped a great and blameless
tomb — we, the sacred army of Argive spearmen —

61
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:28,220
on a jutting headland over the broad Hellespont,
so it might be seen far off, from the open sea,

62
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:30,500
by men now living and by men to come.

63
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,920
And your mother asked the gods for prizes of great beauty

64
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:37,620
and set them in the middle of the field,
for the best of the Achaeans.

65
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:42,600
You have stood by now at the funeral games of many
heroes — when a king has died, and the young men

66
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:48,200
belt themselves and make ready for the prizes —
but had you seen those, your heart would have wondered most:

67
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,760
what beautiful prizes the goddess set out in your honor,

68
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:55,220
silver-footed Thetis. For you were most dear to the gods.

69
00:04:55,220 --> 00:05:02,320
So not even in death did you lose your name: forever,
among all mankind, your fame will be noble, Achilles.

70
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:06,680
But I? What joy of it had I,
when I had wound the war to its end?

71
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:12,800
On my homecoming Zeus devised a wretched death for
me at the hands of Aegisthus and my accursed wife."

72
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:15,240
So they spoke to one another of such things.

73
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:21,000
And the guide came near them, the slayer of Argus,
leading down the souls of the suitors Odysseus broke;

74
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,060
and the two, amazed,
went straight toward them at the sight.

75
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:30,580
And the soul of Agamemnon, Atreus' son,
knew the dear son of Melaneus, far-famed Amphimedon:

76
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:34,060
for he had been his guest-friend, with his home in Ithaca.

77
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,840
The soul of Atreus' son spoke to him first:

78
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:40,800
"Amphimedon, what has happened to you,
that you plunge under the dark earth,

79
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:46,220
all of you picked men, all of an age? A man choosing
the best in a city could not have chosen better.

80
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:50,100
Did Poseidon break you in your ships,
raising cruel winds and long waves?

81
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:55,400
Or did enemies on land destroy you while you were
cutting out their cattle and fine flocks of sheep,

82
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,820
or as they fought for their city and their women?

83
00:05:57,820 --> 00:06:01,200
Answer what I ask: I declare myself your guest-friend.

84
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:06,040
Do you not remember when I came to your house there,
with godlike Menelaus, to urge Odysseus

85
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:09,200
to follow with us to Ilium on the well-benched ships?

86
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:15,820
A whole month it took us to cross all that wide sea,
and hard work to win over Odysseus, sacker of cities."

87
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:18,280
Then the soul of Amphimedon answered him:

88
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:24,800
"Most glorious son of Atreus, Agamemnon lord of men,
I remember all of it, Zeus-nurtured, as you tell it;

89
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,940
and I will recount to you everything, fully and truly —

90
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:31,000
the evil end of our death, how it was brought to pass.

91
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,140
We were courting the wife of Odysseus —
of the man so long away.

92
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:38,960
She neither refused the marriage she
loathed, nor would she make an end,

93
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:43,920
plotting for us death and the black doom;
no — here is another trick her heart devised:

94
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:46,540
she set up a great loom in the halls and wove at it,

95
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:50,520
fine-threaded, beyond all measure wide,
and told us straightway:

96
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:56,480
'Young men, my suitors — since noble Odysseus is dead,
wait, though you burn for my wedding, till I finish

97
00:06:56,480 --> 00:07:02,500
this cloth — let my spinning not be wasted on the wind —
a shroud for the hero Laertes, against the day

98
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:05,680
the ruinous doom of death that lays men low will take him:

99
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:08,640
let no Achaean woman in the land hold it against me

100
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:11,900
that a man who won so much should
lie without his winding-sheet.'

101
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:14,460
So she spoke, and our proud hearts were persuaded.

102
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:21,560
Then every day she wove at the great loom,
and every night she unwove it by torchlight.

103
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,720
Three years her trick escaped us,
and she kept the Achaeans persuaded;

104
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:29,120
but when the fourth year came, as the seasons rolled on,

105
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:36,140
the months waning away, the long tale of days accomplished,
then one of her women, who knew it all, told us,

106
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:38,660
and we caught her unweaving the shining web.

107
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:41,980
So she finished it — against her will, under force.

108
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:48,720
And when she had shown us the robe — the great web woven
and washed — and it shone like the sun or the moon,

109
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:51,680
just then some evil god brought Odysseus from somewhere

110
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:55,520
to the border of the land,
where the swineherd had his house.

111
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:01,260
There came also the dear son of godlike Odysseus,
back in his black ship out of sandy Pylos;

112
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:06,920
and the two of them plotted an evil death for the
suitors and came to the famous town — Odysseus

113
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:10,100
later; it was Telemachus who led the way before him.

114
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:17,300
The swineherd brought the master, in bad clothes on his body,
in the likeness of a beggar, wretched and old,

115
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:20,500
leaning on a stick, with miserable clothing on his body;

116
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,460
and not one of us could know him for the man he was,

117
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:27,080
breaking on us suddenly like that —
not even the older men —

118
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:31,080
no: we went at him with ugly words,
and with things thrown.

119
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:36,520
And he for a while endured it in his own halls,
pelted and taunted, with an enduring heart;

120
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:42,520
but when the mind of Zeus who bears the aegis roused him,
with Telemachus he lifted the beautiful armor away

121
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:47,760
and stowed it in the storeroom and shot the bolts,
and then — his cunning at work — he ordered his wife

122
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:50,940
to set before the suitors the bow and the gray iron,

123
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:55,180
the contest for us star-crossed men,
and the beginning of the slaughter.

124
00:08:55,180 --> 00:09:00,380
And not one of us could stretch the string of
that strong bow: we fell far, far short of it.

125
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:05,500
But when the great bow came into Odysseus' hands,
then all of us together shouted out threats

126
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:11,520
not to give him the bow, however much he pleaded;
only Telemachus urged him on and commanded it.

127
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:15,140
And he took it in his hand, much-enduring noble Odysseus,

128
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:18,060
and strung the bow with ease, and shot through the iron,

129
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:22,260
and went and stood on the threshold,
and poured out the swift arrows,

130
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:25,460
glaring terribly — and he shot Antinous the king.

131
00:09:25,460 --> 00:09:30,800
And then he sent his groaning shafts into the others,
aiming straight ahead; and they fell in heaps.

132
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:33,640
It was plain some god was their helper in the fight:

133
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:38,400
for storming down the hall in their fury they
killed left and right, and hideous groaning rose

134
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:42,300
as heads were smashed, and the
whole floor smoked with blood.

135
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:48,180
So we perished, Agamemnon — and even now our
bodies lie uncared-for in the halls of Odysseus:

136
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:53,640
for the friends in each man's house do not yet know —
the ones who would wash the black gore from our wounds

137
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:57,980
and lay us out with wailing:
for that is the portion of the dead."

138
00:09:57,980 --> 00:10:00,580
Then the soul of Atreus' son answered him:

139
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:07,360
"Blessed son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —
truly you won a wife of great excellence!

140
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:12,840
How sound the mind of blameless Penelope,
Icarius' daughter! How faithfully she kept the memory

141
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:16,660
of Odysseus, her wedded husband!
And so the fame of her excellence

142
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:19,320
will never perish; and the deathless ones will make

143
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:24,180
a song for those on earth, a song of
grace, for steady-hearted Penelope —

144
00:10:24,180 --> 00:10:29,680
not like the daughter of Tyndareus, who plotted evil,
who killed her wedded husband: a song of loathing

145
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:32,840
she will be among mankind; and she brings hard fame

146
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:36,740
on all women — on womankind, even on those who do right."

147
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:39,580
So the two of them spoke to one another of such things,

148
00:10:39,580 --> 00:10:43,300
standing in the house of Hades,
under the hidden places of the earth.

149
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:46,580
And the others — when they had gone
down from the city, they soon reached

150
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:49,920
the beautiful well-worked farm
of Laertes, which Laertes himself

151
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:53,120
had won long ago, when his labors for it were many.

152
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:58,280
There was his house, and running all around it the
sheds where the bound slaves ate, and sat, and slept —

153
00:10:58,280 --> 00:10:59,820
the ones whose labor did his will.

154
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:03,020
And in the house was an old Sicilian woman, who cared

155
00:11:03,020 --> 00:11:06,660
for the old man devotedly,
out on the farm far from the town.

156
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,500
And there Odysseus spoke to the slaves and to his son:

157
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:15,440
"You now — go inside the well-built house,
and slaughter for dinner, right away, the best of the pigs;

158
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:20,240
and I will make a trial of my father — whether he
will know me, whether his eyes will make me out,

159
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:22,700
or fail to know a son so long away."

160
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:25,580
So speaking he handed the slaves his weapons of war.

161
00:11:25,580 --> 00:11:30,780
They went quickly on into the house; and Odysseus
moved up toward the fruited orchard, testing.

162
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:33,800
And going down into the great garden he found no Dolius,

163
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:38,860
none of the slaves, none of the sons: they had gone
off gathering fieldstones for the orchard wall,

164
00:11:38,860 --> 00:11:41,120
and the old man had led them the way.

165
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:47,360
So he found his father alone in the well-worked orchard,
spading round a young tree — in a filthy tunic,

166
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:52,720
patched, disgraceful; round his shins he had bound
stitched leggings of oxhide, to fend off the scratches,

167
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:59,220
and gloves on his hands because of the thorns;
and on his head he wore a goatskin cap — feeding his sorrow.

168
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:04,960
And when much-enduring noble Odysseus saw him —
broken with old age, the great grief packed inside him —

169
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:08,260
he stopped beneath a towering pear tree, and wept.

170
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:10,520
Then he weighed it in his mind and in his heart:

171
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:14,020
to kiss his father, and hold him,
and tell him everything —

172
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:16,980
how he had come, how he had reached his fathers' land —

173
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:20,240
or question him first, and test him through and through.

174
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:22,660
And as he weighed it, this seemed to him the better way:

175
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:25,020
to try him first with words that bite.

176
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:28,440
With this in mind, noble Odysseus went straight up to him.

177
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:34,220
He was holding his head down, spading round his tree,
and standing beside him his shining son spoke to him:

178
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:38,620
"Old man — no lack of skill in you at
tending an orchard! Not one thing —

179
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:45,820
no plant, no fig tree, no vine, no olive, no pear tree,
no vegetable bed — goes untended in this garden.

180
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:49,260
But another thing I will say —
and do not take anger into your heart:

181
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:52,920
you — you are not well kept. Hard old age is on you,

182
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:56,520
and with it you are shabby with
grime, and clothed in disgrace.

183
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:59,140
It cannot be for idleness that your master neglects you;

184
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:01,420
and there is nothing of the slave to be seen in you,

185
00:13:01,420 --> 00:13:04,680
in your face or your stature: you look like a kingly man.

186
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:06,880
You look like one who, after his bath and his dinner,

187
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:10,260
should sleep on softness:
for that is the right of the old.

188
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:12,840
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

189
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:15,180
Whose slave are you? Whose orchard do you tend?

190
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:18,340
And tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

191
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:24,040
is this land we have come to truly Ithaca,
as that man told me who met me on the road just now —

192
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:29,720
not too well furnished in the wits: he had not the
patience to tell me everything or hear out my question

193
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:36,000
about a guest-friend of mine — whether he lives still,
or is dead by now, and down in the house of Hades.

194
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,700
For I will tell you this; mark it and hear me:

195
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:45,400
I once played host, in my own dear country, to a man
who came to our place; and never has another mortal

196
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:49,080
of all the far-come strangers
been more welcome in my house.

197
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:55,100
He declared his stock was out of Ithaca;
he said his father was Laertes, Arceisius' son.

198
00:13:55,100 --> 00:14:01,160
So I brought him to the house and gave him welcome,
warm and openhanded — the house had plenty in it —

199
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:04,300
and I gave him gifts of friendship, such as fitted:

200
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:09,880
seven talents of well-worked gold I gave him,
a mixing-bowl all silver, worked with flowers,

201
00:14:09,880 --> 00:14:15,240
twelve single cloaks, as many coverlets,
as many beautiful mantles, tunics with them,

202
00:14:15,240 --> 00:14:17,980
and besides all this, four women skilled in blameless

203
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:21,740
handwork, good-looking ones —
his own choice, whichever he would."

204
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:24,320
And his father answered him, weeping:

205
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:31,580
"Stranger, you have come indeed to the land you ask for —
but violent men hold it now, men lost to decency.

206
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:35,240
The gifts you showered on him —
the countless gifts — were wasted.

207
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:40,440
If you had found him alive in the land of Ithaca,
he would have sent you off well matched in gifts,

208
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:44,620
well hosted too: that is the law,
for whoever begins the kindness.

209
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:47,400
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

210
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:54,480
how many years is it since you hosted him — that guest
of yours, that luckless man — my son, if he ever was:

211
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:58,660
my ill-starred son? Whom, far from
his friends and his fathers' land,

212
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:01,520
either the fish have eaten in the sea, or on dry ground

213
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:04,640
he has become the spoil of
beasts and birds; and his mother

214
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:08,720
never shrouded and mourned him,
nor his father — we who bore him;

215
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:12,460
nor did his richly dowered wife, steady-hearted Penelope,

216
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:15,520
keen over her own husband on the bier, as was fitting,

217
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:18,360
or close his eyes: and that is the portion of the dead.

218
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:21,800
And tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

219
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:25,600
What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?

220
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,840
And where is the fast ship standing that brought you here

221
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:30,720
with your godlike companions? Or did you come a passenger

222
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:33,780
on another's ship, which set you down and went on?"

223
00:15:33,780 --> 00:15:36,300
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

224
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:38,420
"Then I will tell you everything exactly.

225
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:41,540
I am from Alybas, where I have my famous house:

226
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:46,800
son of Apheidas, the son of lord Polypemon;
my own name is Eperitus. But a god

227
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:50,140
drove me wandering here from Sicania, against my will,

228
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:53,420
and my ship stands off the fields, away from the town.

229
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:58,460
As for Odysseus — this is now the fifth year
since he went from there, and left my country —

230
00:15:58,460 --> 00:16:01,740
the ill-starred man! And yet the
birds were good when he set out:

231
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:05,120
on the right; and I rejoiced in them as I sent him off,

232
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:06,880
And the heart in both of us hoped

233
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:11,220
we would meet again as guest-friends,
and give each other shining gifts."

234
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:15,000
So he spoke; and a black cloud
of grief closed over the old man:

235
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,540
with both hands he scooped up the scorched gray dust

236
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:22,160
and poured it down over his white head,
sobbing thick and fast.

237
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,920
And the heart of Odysseus was shaken;
up through his nostrils

238
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:29,320
shot the sharp sting of tears —
his father before his eyes.

239
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:32,960
He sprang to him, and caught
him in his arms, and kissed him:

240
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:39,680
"Father — I am he. I myself, the man you ask for,
come home in the twentieth year to my fathers' land.

241
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:43,600
Stop now — no more weeping, no more tears and mourning.

242
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:46,920
For I tell you this — and the need to hurry is very great:

243
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:49,240
I have killed the suitors in our own halls;

244
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:53,040
I have punished their heart-stinging
outrage and their evil deeds."

245
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:55,760
Then Laertes answered him and said:

246
00:16:55,760 --> 00:17:03,220
"If you are truly Odysseus — my son — come home,
tell me some sign, some plain one, so I can believe."

247
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:05,920
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

248
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:11,800
"First — this scar: see it with your own eyes —
where the boar dealt me its white tusk, on Parnassus,

249
00:17:11,800 --> 00:17:15,440
the time I went there. It was you
who sent me — you and my honored

250
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:18,680
mother — to Autolycus, her dear father, to collect

251
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:22,560
the gifts he promised me,
and nodded to, when he came here.

252
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:25,780
And then — come — let me tell you
the trees in the well-worked orchard

253
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:28,780
you gave me once. I was begging you for each of them,

254
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:31,640
a little boy, trailing you through the garden. We wound

255
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:34,720
among these very trees, and you named them one by one:

256
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:41,360
you gave me thirteen pear trees, and ten apple trees,
and forty fig trees; and you named fifty rows of vines

257
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:47,700
as mine to come, each one ripening in its own turn —
clusters of every kind hanging along them —

258
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:51,500
whenever the seasons of Zeus
came down and weighed them low."

259
00:17:51,500 --> 00:17:56,260
So he spoke; and the old man's knees went
slack where he stood, and his dear heart,

260
00:17:56,260 --> 00:17:59,900
as he recognized the tokens Odysseus described so surely.

261
00:17:59,900 --> 00:18:02,700
He threw his arms around his dear son — and fainting

262
00:18:02,700 --> 00:18:05,980
he was caught against him by much-enduring noble Odysseus.

263
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:10,060
And when he had got his breath,
and the spirit gathered back into his heart,

264
00:18:10,060 --> 00:18:12,300
once more he found his words, and answered:

265
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:15,960
"Father Zeus — you gods are still there on high Olympus,

266
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:19,160
if truly the suitors have paid for their reckless outrage!

267
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:25,360
But now the terror in my heart is this: that soon
the men of Ithaca will all come here against us,

268
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:29,280
and send out messengers everywhere,
to every Cephallenian town."

269
00:18:29,280 --> 00:18:31,920
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

270
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:35,280
"Courage. Do not let that
trouble the heart in your breast.

271
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:38,700
Come — let us go to the house
that stands here by the orchard:

272
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:44,520
I sent Telemachus ahead there, with the cowherd and the
swineherd, to make the meal as quickly as they can."

273
00:18:44,520 --> 00:18:48,060
So the two of them talked,
and made their way to the beautiful house.

274
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:53,480
And when they came to the well-settled house,
they found Telemachus and the cowherd and the swineherd

275
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:57,060
carving meat in plenty and mixing the sparkling wine.

276
00:18:57,060 --> 00:18:59,900
Meanwhile the great-hearted Laertes, in his own house,

277
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:03,780
was bathed by the Sicilian serving-woman,
and rubbed with oil,

278
00:19:03,780 --> 00:19:06,660
and she threw a beautiful cloak around him; and Athena

279
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:10,060
came and stood close by,
and filled out the limbs of the shepherd

280
00:19:10,060 --> 00:19:14,680
of the people: taller than before
she made him, and broader to the eye.

281
00:19:14,680 --> 00:19:21,180
Out of the bath he came; and his dear son wondered at him,
seeing him face to face like the deathless gods,

282
00:19:21,180 --> 00:19:24,440
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

283
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:31,340
"Father — surely one of the gods who are forever has
made you finer to look at, in beauty and in stature!"

284
00:19:31,340 --> 00:19:33,820
Then Laertes the clear-headed answered him:

285
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:40,240
"If only — father Zeus, Athena, Apollo! —
such a man as I was when I took Nericus, the strong-built

286
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:44,040
citadel on the mainland cape,
lording it over the Cephallenians —

287
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:51,760
such a man, in armor, I had stood beside you yesterday
in our own house, and beaten at the suitor-men:

288
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:54,340
then I would have unstrung the knees of many of them

289
00:19:54,340 --> 00:19:57,960
there in the halls — and the heart
in you would have been glad!"

290
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:00,280
So they spoke to one another of such things.

291
00:20:00,280 --> 00:20:03,220
And when the others had finished
their work and made the meal,

292
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:06,340
they sat down in order on the benches and the chairs,

293
00:20:06,340 --> 00:20:08,980
and were just putting their
hands to the food, when up came

294
00:20:08,980 --> 00:20:12,980
the old man Dolius, and with him the old man's sons,

295
00:20:12,980 --> 00:20:15,840
tired out from their field-work: their mother had gone out

296
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:18,920
and called them — the old Sicilian woman, who raised them

297
00:20:18,920 --> 00:20:23,100
and tended the old man devotedly,
now that age had hold of him.

298
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:29,200
And when they saw Odysseus, and their hearts took it in,
they stopped in the hall, astounded. But Odysseus

299
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:31,640
fastened on them with gentle words and said:

300
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:34,700
"Old man — sit down to dinner,
and all of you, forget your wonder.

301
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:37,540
We have been waiting in the hall a long while, eager

302
00:20:37,540 --> 00:20:41,060
to put our hands to the bread,
expecting you every minute."

303
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:44,520
So he spoke; and Dolius came straight at him, both arms

304
00:20:44,520 --> 00:20:48,480
spread wide; and he caught Odysseus'
hand and kissed it at the wrist,

305
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:51,360
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words:

306
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:54,020
"Dear master — since you have
come home to us who longed for you

307
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:57,800
and had given up believing,
and the gods themselves have brought you:

308
00:20:57,800 --> 00:21:01,180
health, and all joy to you,
and may the gods give you happiness!

309
00:21:01,180 --> 00:21:03,580
But tell me this thing truly, so that I may know it:

310
00:21:03,580 --> 00:21:08,880
does watchful-minded Penelope know already that
you are home — or shall we send a messenger?"

311
00:21:08,880 --> 00:21:11,780
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

312
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:15,540
"Old man, she knows already.
Why should you be busied with that?"

313
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:18,960
So he spoke; and Dolius sat
back down on the polished stool.

314
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:22,900
And in the same way the sons of
Dolius, around famous Odysseus,

315
00:21:22,900 --> 00:21:28,320
pressed his hands and welcomed him with words,
and sat down in order beside Dolius their father.

316
00:21:28,320 --> 00:21:30,820
So they were busy with dinner in the house.

317
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:34,180
But Rumor the messenger went
darting through the city everywhere,

318
00:21:34,180 --> 00:21:37,120
crying the hateful death and doom of the suitors.

319
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:39,400
And the people heard it all at once, and gathered

320
00:21:39,400 --> 00:21:43,700
from every side, with groans and wailing,
before the house of Odysseus.

321
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:47,180
Each family carried its dead out
of the house, and buried them;

322
00:21:47,180 --> 00:21:52,420
and the dead of the other cities they sent home,
laying each man aboard the fishermen's fast ships;

323
00:21:52,420 --> 00:21:56,240
and they went in a body to the
assembly-place, grieving at heart.

324
00:21:56,240 --> 00:22:00,820
And when they were assembled, all gathered together,
Eupeithes rose among them and spoke:

325
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:04,060
for a grief there was no forgetting lay upon his heart —

326
00:22:04,060 --> 00:22:10,680
for his son, Antinous, the first man noble Odysseus killed;
weeping for him he rose and spoke among them:

327
00:22:10,680 --> 00:22:14,720
"Friends — a monstrous work this man
has wrought against the Achaeans!

328
00:22:14,720 --> 00:22:21,120
Some he led off in the ships — many men, and brave ones —
and he lost the hollow ships, and lost the people;

329
00:22:21,120 --> 00:22:25,400
and some he has killed on his return —
far the best of the Cephallenians.

330
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:31,220
Come then — before he can get away quickly to Pylos,
or to shining Elis, where the Epeians hold power —

331
00:22:31,220 --> 00:22:35,320
let us move! Or we hang our heads in shame forever after.

332
00:22:35,320 --> 00:22:40,920
This is a disgrace even for men unborn to hear of,
if we do not take vengeance on the murderers of our sons

333
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:44,360
and our brothers. For me —
there would be no sweetness left

334
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:48,020
in living: I would rather die at
once and be among the perished.

335
00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:51,420
Move — before they get across the water and away from us!"

336
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:55,540
So he spoke, weeping; and pity
took hold of all the Achaeans.

337
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:58,360
But now Medon came up to them, and the godlike singer,

338
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:01,920
out of the halls of Odysseus, for sleep had let them go;

339
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:05,420
they stood in the middle of them;
and amazement took every man.

340
00:23:05,420 --> 00:23:08,740
And Medon, whose mind knew wisdom, spoke among them:

341
00:23:08,740 --> 00:23:14,860
"Hear me now, men of Ithaca. Not without the will of
the deathless gods did Odysseus devise these things.

342
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:21,680
I myself saw a deathless god, who stood close beside
Odysseus, and looked in all things like Mentor;

343
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:29,140
and this deathless god now showed in front of Odysseus,
heartening him — and now he stormed the hall,

344
00:23:29,140 --> 00:23:32,600
stampeding the suitors; and they fell in heaps."

345
00:23:32,600 --> 00:23:36,720
So he spoke; and the pale terror took hold of them all.

346
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:43,480
And the old hero Halitherses spoke among them,
Mastor's son — for he alone saw before and behind:

347
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:46,960
with good will toward them he spoke out and said:

348
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:50,060
"Hear me now, men of Ithaca, hear what I say.

349
00:23:50,060 --> 00:23:53,520
By your own fault, friends,
these things have come to pass:

350
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:57,180
you would not listen to me,
nor to Mentor, shepherd of the people,

351
00:23:57,180 --> 00:24:00,600
and make your sons leave off their reckless folly —

352
00:24:00,600 --> 00:24:03,740
your sons who did a monstrous
work in their wicked blindness,

353
00:24:03,740 --> 00:24:06,120
shearing away the goods and dishonoring the wife

354
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:10,020
of a man of the first rank,
saying he would come home no more.

355
00:24:10,020 --> 00:24:13,020
So now — let it rest here. Do as I say:

356
00:24:13,020 --> 00:24:17,220
do not march. Some of us may draw
the evil down on his own head."

357
00:24:17,220 --> 00:24:22,900
So he spoke; and more than half leapt up with a great
battle-shout — but the rest stayed there in a body:

358
00:24:22,900 --> 00:24:26,640
for the word did not please them
in their hearts; it was Eupeithes

359
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:30,160
they followed; and they rushed at once for their weapons.

360
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:33,180
And when they had cased their
flesh in the flashing bronze,

361
00:24:33,180 --> 00:24:38,860
they gathered in a body before the wide-spaced town;
and Eupeithes led them, in his folly:

362
00:24:38,860 --> 00:24:42,240
he was sure he would avenge the
killing of his son — and he was never

363
00:24:42,240 --> 00:24:45,700
to come back again: he was to meet his own fate there.

364
00:24:45,700 --> 00:24:49,220
And now Athena spoke to Zeus, the son of Cronos:

365
00:24:49,220 --> 00:24:52,860
"Father of us all, son of Cronos, highest of the mighty,

366
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:56,800
answer me when I ask: what is your mind hiding now within?

367
00:24:56,800 --> 00:25:03,140
Will you push on with evil war and the terrible
battle-din, or set the two sides now in friendship?"

368
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:05,940
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered her:

369
00:25:05,940 --> 00:25:09,760
"My child, why do you ask me? Why question me on this?

370
00:25:09,760 --> 00:25:15,560
Was it not you who framed this plan yourself —
that Odysseus should come home and pay those men in full?

371
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:18,880
Do as you will. But I will tell you what is fitting.

372
00:25:18,880 --> 00:25:25,660
Now that noble Odysseus has paid the suitors out,
let them cut faithful oaths — he to be king all his days —

373
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:31,600
and let us, for our part, set a forgetting of the killing
of sons and of brothers. Let them love one another

374
00:25:31,600 --> 00:25:35,580
as before; and let wealth and peace be there in plenty."

375
00:25:35,580 --> 00:25:42,460
So speaking he stirred Athena — eager long before —
and down she went darting from the peaks of Olympus.

376
00:25:42,460 --> 00:25:46,040
Now when they had put from them
the desire for honey-hearted food,

377
00:25:46,040 --> 00:25:49,020
much-enduring noble Odysseus began among them:

378
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:53,320
"Someone go out and look —
in case they are close, and coming."

379
00:25:53,320 --> 00:25:57,220
So he spoke; and a son of Dolius went out, as he ordered,

380
00:25:57,220 --> 00:26:01,080
and stood on the threshold,
and saw them — all of them — near,

381
00:26:01,080 --> 00:26:03,860
and quickly he spoke winged words to Odysseus:

382
00:26:03,860 --> 00:26:07,400
"Here they are — close! Quick — to arms!"

383
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:12,860
Up they sprang and got into their armor —
the four around Odysseus, and the six sons of Dolius;

384
00:26:12,860 --> 00:26:18,040
and with them Laertes and Dolius got
into armor too, gray though they were.

385
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:21,700
And when they had cased their
flesh in the flashing bronze,

386
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:25,000
they opened the doors and went out; and Odysseus led.

387
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:30,360
And near them came the daughter of Zeus,
Athena, likened to Mentor in body and in voice;

388
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:33,840
and much-enduring noble Odysseus
was glad at the sight of her,

389
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:36,980
and quickly he spoke to Telemachus his dear son:

390
00:26:36,980 --> 00:26:40,500
"Telemachus — now you will learn
it for yourself, standing where

391
00:26:40,500 --> 00:26:43,820
men fight it out, and the best are sorted from the rest:

392
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:47,460
shame nothing of your fathers' line.
In strength and manhood

393
00:26:47,460 --> 00:26:50,860
we have excelled from of old across the whole earth."

394
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:53,400
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

395
00:26:53,400 --> 00:26:56,620
"You will see me, if you care to look,
dear father, in this temper

396
00:26:56,620 --> 00:27:00,060
shaming nothing of your line — as you have said."

397
00:27:00,060 --> 00:27:03,560
So he spoke; and Laertes exulted, and cried out:

398
00:27:03,560 --> 00:27:07,440
"What a day this is for me, dear gods! Great is my joy:

399
00:27:07,440 --> 00:27:11,380
my son and my son's son are striving over excellence!"

400
00:27:11,380 --> 00:27:14,120
And gray-eyed Athena came and stood by him and said:

401
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:18,860
"Son of Arceisius — far the dearest of all my companions —

402
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:26,580
pray to the gray-eyed girl, and to Zeus her father,
then swing the long-shadowed spear up, and let it fly."

403
00:27:26,580 --> 00:27:30,600
So spoke Pallas Athena,
and breathed great strength into him;

404
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:37,060
and he prayed then to the daughter of great Zeus,
and swung the long-shadowed spear up, and let it fly,

405
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:40,120
and struck Eupeithes through the bronze-cheeked helmet:

406
00:27:40,120 --> 00:27:43,820
the helmet did not stop the spear;
the point drove through,

407
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:48,260
and he fell with a crash,
and his armor clattered upon him.

408
00:27:48,260 --> 00:27:54,540
And Odysseus and his shining son fell on the front-fighters,
hacking with swords and double-pointed spears;

409
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:58,280
and they would have destroyed them all,
cut them off from homecoming,

410
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:05,560
had not Athena, daughter of Zeus who bears the aegis,
cried out with her voice, and checked the whole array:

411
00:28:05,560 --> 00:28:12,720
"Hold back, men of Ithaca, from grinding war —
separate now, quickly, and without blood!"

412
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:16,260
So spoke Athena; and the pale terror seized them:

413
00:28:16,260 --> 00:28:21,500
out of their frightened hands the weapons flew,
and all fell on the ground at the goddess's cry,

414
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:25,000
and they turned toward the city, aching to live.

415
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,980
And terribly then cried out much-enduring noble Odysseus,

416
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:31,860
and gathering himself he swooped like a high-flown eagle.

417
00:28:31,860 --> 00:28:35,300
But then the son of Cronos let fly his smoking bolt,

418
00:28:35,300 --> 00:28:39,480
and it fell in front of the gray-eyed one,
the great Father's daughter.

419
00:28:39,480 --> 00:28:42,220
And then gray-eyed Athena said to Odysseus:

420
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:49,660
"Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many
devices, hold! Stop the strife of leveling war —

421
00:28:49,660 --> 00:28:54,420
for fear the son of Cronos be angered
with you: Zeus of the wide voice."

422
00:28:54,420 --> 00:28:58,540
So spoke Athena. He obeyed — and his heart was glad.

423
00:28:58,540 --> 00:29:02,540
And a sworn peace between the
two sides, for all days to come,

424
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:10,896
was set by Pallas Athena, daughter of Zeus who bears
the aegis, likened to Mentor in body and in voice.
